   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Dynamical Multi-Head Attention\n",
    "\n",
    "In this notebook I will prototype the simplest possible version of the TCA system: a randomly instantiated multi-head attention system that iteratively updates the states of several \"particles\" (tokens) in a differential manner. \n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to start with the simplest possible *implementation* -- not necessarily the simplest possible theoretical formulations -- in the hopes that it will facilitate faster prototyping and insight. I'm not trying to make the code here pretty or expandable. The only goal is to get *something* built. Further iterations will re-build with better practices. \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## TODO\n",
    " - [ ] Set up `local attention` -- literally just windowed attention. See if you can make it correspond to XYZ position... [Link](https://github.com/lucidrains/local-attention)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 204,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Import box\n",
    "import numpy as np \n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "import torch \n",
    "from torch import nn\n",
    "\n",
    "from tqdm import tqdm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1: State Variable Setup\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's start by defining the dimensionalities for the particle states and randomly initializing a state matrix accordingly."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 205,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Setting up the MHA \n",
    "embed_dim = 5   # Key and query dimension expected in the input.\n",
    "key_dim = 5     # Expected dimension of the keys(?)\n",
    "val_dim = 5     # Expected dimension of the \n",
    "num_heads = 1   # Number of heads in the MHA.\n",
    "multihead_attn = nn.MultiheadAttention(embed_dim, num_heads, kdim=key_dim, vdim=val_dim, bias=False) # value/output dimension should be the same as `embed_dim` by default"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 206,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "torch.Size([1000, 1, 5])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 206,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "## Setting up the state matrix \n",
    "L = 100             # Number of particles/sequence length\n",
    "Eq = embed_dim      # Dimension of the states/queries/keys/values\n",
    "num_batch = 1\n",
    "\n",
    "state_matrix = torch.rand(L, num_batch, Eq)\n",
    "state_matrix.shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 207,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "mha_out[0] shape:  torch.Size([100, 1, 5]) \t(Output values)\n",
      "mha_out[1] shape:  torch.Size([1, 100, 100]) \t(Attention map)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "dx, attn_map = multihead_attn(state_matrix, state_matrix, state_matrix)\n",
    "print(\"mha_out[0] shape: \", mha_out[0].shape, \"\\t(Output values)\")\n",
    "print(\"mha_out[1] shape: \", mha_out[1].shape, \"\\t(Attention map)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 208,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Visualizing the states \n",
    "def visualize_state_matrix(X_, title='State Visualization: XY-RGB State Interpretation', savename='figs/test.png'):\n",
    "    X = X_.detach()\n",
    "    XY = X[:, :2]\n",
    "    RGB = X[:,2:5]\n",
    "    plt.scatter(XY[:,0], XY[:,1], c=RGB)\n",
    "    plt.title(title)\n",
    "    plt.savefig(savename)\n",
    "    plt.close()\n",
    "\n",
    "visualize_state_matrix(state_matrix[:,0,:])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 209,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<matplotlib.image.AxesImage at 0x3fc252b50>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 209,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(attn_map[0,:,:].detach().numpy(), cmap='gray')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 210,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Input state matrix: \t tensor(0.4950)  +/-  tensor(0.2893)\n",
      "MHA output matrix: \t tensor(-0.1737)  +/-  tensor(0.1336)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "## Before moving on, let's get a sense of the means/variance of the input vs. output\n",
    "sm_std, sm_mean = torch.std_mean(state_matrix)\n",
    "dx_std, dx_mean = torch.std_mean(dx.detach())\n",
    "print(\"Input state matrix: \\t\", sm_mean, \" +/- \", sm_std)\n",
    "print(\"MHA output matrix: \\t\", dx_mean, \" +/- \", dx_std)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`Finding`: It looks like PyTorch doesn't (by default) support output `value` dimension being different than the input `embedding` dimension. I think that should be fine though, since we should be able to just re-project the output `value` into the desired dimension with a linear projection or a FFNN."
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2: Frame Generation\n",
    "\n",
    "In order to get something \"great\" done in \"90 minutes\", let's just render the frames into `figs/anim01` as we apply the untrained/uninitialized update system that also has the ability to mutate position/color arbitrarily. \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "*Don't worry, we'll eventually make it more constrained!*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 211,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Define a for loop that iterates thru some number of state updates\n",
    "num_iters = 1000\n",
    "dt = 0.2\n",
    "fig_folder='anim03'"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 212,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "100%|██████████| 1000/1000 [01:31<00:00, 10.92it/s]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "for i in tqdm(range(num_iters)): \n",
    "    dx, _ = multihead_attn(state_matrix, state_matrix, state_matrix)\n",
    "    dxy = dx[:,:,0:2]\n",
    "\n",
    "    # let's try normalizing dxy -- right now the variance is super tight.\n",
    "    dxy_std, dxy_mean = torch.std_mean(dxy)\n",
    "    dxy = dxy - dxy_mean\n",
    "    dxy = dxy/dxy_std\n",
    "\n",
    "    dxyrgb = torch.zeros(dx.shape) \n",
    "    dxyrgb[:,:,0:2] = dxy\n",
    "    state_matrix += dxyrgb*dt\n",
    "    \n",
    "\n",
    "    visualize_state_matrix(state_matrix[:,0,:], savename=f'figs/{fig_folder}/frame_{str(i).zfill(3)}.png')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Seems like relatively uninteresting behavior from the randomly initialized MHA... it might be worth implementing a simple MHA layer with some locality masking on the attention heads from scratch just to get *some* interesting collective behaviour."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 213,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<matplotlib.image.AxesImage at 0x2e3a68250>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 213,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.imshow(dxyrgb[:,0,:].detach().numpy())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 215,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "[<matplotlib.lines.Line2D at 0x2927581c0>]"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 215,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.plot(dxyrgb[:,0,0].detach().numpy())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3: Building the Video\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's make a function that, based on an input `path` to the folders with all the `png` files, creates a video with the same name as the enclosing folder. Let's use opencv for this."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 218,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "anim03\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import cv2\n",
    "import glob\n",
    "print(fig_folder)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 219,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "png_path = f'figs/{fig_folder}/*.png'\n",
    "img_array = []\n",
    "for filename in sorted(glob.glob(png_path)): \n",
    "    img = cv2.imread(filename)\n",
    "    height, width, layers = img.shape\n",
    "    size = (width, height)\n",
    "    img_array.append(img)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 220,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "OpenCV: FFMPEG: tag 0x58564944/'DIVX' is not supported with codec id 12 and format 'mp4 / MP4 (MPEG-4 Part 14)'\n",
      "OpenCV: FFMPEG: fallback to use tag 0x7634706d/'mp4v'\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "out = cv2.VideoWriter(f'figs/{fig_folder}.mp4', cv2.VideoWriter_fourcc(*'DIVX'), 15, size)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 221,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "for i in range(len(img_array)):\n",
    "    out.write(img_array[i])\n",
    "out.release()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4: Thoughts + Next Steps\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
